<worksheet>
    <title>LT 10 Half-Life and Regression</title>
    <page>
    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>.  
                A storage tank contains a radioactive element.  Let <m>P=f(t)</m> be the percentage of the element that reamains at <m>t</m> years since the element was placed in the tank. The graph of <m>f(t)</m> is shown below.
            </p>
        <image source="half-life.jpeg" width="40%"> 
            <shortdescription>half life starting of 10 years starting at 100 percent</shortdescription>
        </image>
        </introduction>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                What is the half-life of the element?
                        </p>
        </statement>
    </task> 
    <task workspace="0.01 in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Create a table of values for <m>P(t)</m> for <m>t=0, 10, 20, 30, 40</m>, for the percentage of the element that remains at <m>t</m> years since the element was placed in the tank. 
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.01 in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Write the function <m>P(t)</m> that models the percentage of the element that remains at <m>t</m> years since the element was placed in the tank.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
        <task workspace="0.01 in">
            <statement> 
                <p>
                    Find <m>P(55)</m> and <m>P(100)</m>.  What do these values mean in the context of the problem?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
       </exercise>
    </page>
    <page>
    <exercise workspace="0.01 in">
        <introduction>
         <sidebyside widths="70% 30%" valigntop="true">
            <p>
                When you have sex with someone, you are having sex with everyone they and their partners have had sex with for the last ten years.  Many sexually active people are indirectly exposed to more than one sexual partner each year because their partner has had sex with someone else.  In this activity, we are going to see what that looks like in numbers.  
              </p>
                
                       
            <image source="sexExpose.png" width="30%">
            <shortdescription>image depicting sexual partners growing exponentially</shortdescription>
            </image>
            </sidebyside>
            <p>
            The table below shows the number of people exposed to a sexually transmitted disease based on the number of partners a person has had sex with in the last ten years.  The data is based on the assumption that each partner has had sex with two other people in the last ten years.
            </p> 
           <tabular top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
             <col width="30%" halign="center"/>
             <col width="30%" halign="center"/>>
            <row header="yes" halign="center" >
                <cell>Number of Partners</cell>
                <cell>Number of People Exposed</cell>
            </row>
            <row halign="center">
                <cell>1</cell>
                <cell>1</cell>
            </row>
            <row halign="center">
                <cell>2</cell>
                <cell>3</cell>
            </row>
            <row halign="center">
                <cell>3</cell>
                <cell>7</cell>
            </row>
            <row halign="center">           
                <cell>4</cell>
                <cell>15</cell>
            </row>
            <row halign="center">
                <cell>5</cell>
                <cell>31</cell>
            </row>
            <row halign="center">
                <cell>6</cell>
                <cell>63</cell>
            </row>
            <row halign="center">
                <cell>7</cell>
                <cell>127</cell>
            </row>
            <row halign="center">
                <cell>8</cell>
                <cell>255</cell>
            </row>
            <row halign="center">
                <cell>9</cell>
                <cell>511</cell>
            </row>
            <row halign="center">
                <cell>10</cell>
                <cell>1023</cell>
            </row>          
        </tabular>
       
           
        </introduction> 
        <task workspace="0.01 in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find a LINEAR regression model for the data in the table above.  Is it a good fit?  Why or why not?           
            </p>
            <p> Look at the graph of the data.  How does the data fit the model?  </p> 
            <p> Is it a good fit?  Why or why not? </p> 
        </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find an EXPONENTIAL regression model for the data in the table above. (Choose Exponential Regression and click on Log Mode) </p> 
                <p> Look at the graph of the data.  How does the data fit the model? </p>
                <p> Is it a good fit?  Why or why not?   
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        
        </exercise>
       </page>
       <page>
        <exercise>
        <task workspace="0.01 in"> 
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Use both regression equations(not the table), individually,  to find the number of people someone would be exposed to if they both had 12 partners.  SHOW WORK.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task workspace="0.01 in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Did the answers above, verify your choice of better model?  Why or why not?              </p>
               
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task workspace="0.01 in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Use the best equation, a table, and a graph, to find how many partners a person would have if they were exposed to 10,000 people?   Answer the question in a sentence.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>
   </page>
</worksheet>